    <body>
      <sec id="article-140354.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Vascular anomalies of the orbit represent a heterogeneous group of vascular lesions found within the orbit. A profound understanding of the natural course, appropriate nomenclature, clinical features, and imaging characteristics is essential for accurate diagnosis and appropriate management. This activity reviews the recently laid-out guidelines by the International Society for the Study of Vascular Anomalies (ISSVA) and describes the evaluation and management of OVAs. It also highlights the role of an interprofessional team-driven approach to deliver optimal outcomes.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Outline the recent ISSVA classification of vascular anomalies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Describe the diverse clinical spectrum of orbital vascular anomalies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Review the radiological characteristics of the vascular lesions of the orbit.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Explain the appropriate management approach for orbital vascular anomalies.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=140354&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=140354">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Orbital vascular anomalies (OVAs) comprise a diverse spectrum of lesions encountered within the orbit. Owing to the complex anatomy of the orbit, the controversial classification schemes for vascular anomalies, the variable clinical behavior of each subtype, and the limited understanding of the natural course of the disease, the management of OVAs is challenging. A meticulous evaluation of relevant imaging modalities and a coordinated, stepwise approach are crucial for establishing an accurate diagnosis and initiating an appropriate treatment strategy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r1">[1]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Classification of Vascular Anomalies</p>
        <p>Several classification systems for vascular anomalies have been proposed and refined over time as understanding of endothelial biology, imaging, and interventional techniques has evolved.</p>
        <p>Classification Based on Endothelial Proliferative Activity (Mulliken and Glowacki, 1982)</p>
        <p>Mulliken and Glowacki classified vascular anomalies according to endothelial proliferative activity, dividing them into hemangiomas and vascular malformations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r1">[1]</xref> This framework remains foundational to modern classification systems.</p>
        <p>Hemangiomas</p>
        <p>Hemangiomas are true vascular tumors characterized by increased endothelial mitotic activity. They typically follow a predictable clinical course with&#x000a0;2 distinct phases: proliferative and involuting.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Proliferative phase</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Endothelial hyperplasia with increased [&#x000b3;H]-thymidine incorporation</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Presence of a multilaminated basement membrane beneath the endothelium</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rapid lesion growth, most commonly during early infancy</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Involuting phase</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive fibrosis and fatty deposition on histology</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low to absent thymidine labeling</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Initial rapid growth followed by gradual regression</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Vascular Malformations Vascular malformations are structural anomalies of blood vessels characterized by normal endothelial turnover. These lesions are composed of abnormal capillary, venous, lymphatic, arterial, or combined vascular elements and generally grow proportionally with the patient without spontaneous regression.</p>
        <p>Classification Based on Hemodynamic Properties (Orbital Society, 1999)</p>
        <p>The Orbital Society proposed a classification system based on lesion hemodynamics, dividing vascular anomalies into:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>No-flow lesions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Venous (low-flow) lesions</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Arterial (high-flow) lesions&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r3">[3]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Rootman&#x02019;s Hemodynamic Model of Orbital Vascular Malformations</p>
        <p>Rootman further refined hemodynamic classification by describing&#x000a0;3 major types of orbital vascular malformations:</p>
        <p>Type 1: No-flow lesions</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>These lesions have no communication with the venous system and include combined venous-lymphatic malformations, which have historically been misclassified as lymphangiomas.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Type 2: Low-flow (venous) lesions</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>These lesions have direct communication with the venous system and are further subdivided into:<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Distensible lesions with rich venous communication</p></list-item><list-item><p>Non-distensible lesions with limited venous communication</p></list-item><list-item><p>Combined lesions with features of both</p></list-item></list></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Type 3: High-flow (arterial) lesions</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>These include arteriovenous malformations with direct arterial inflow and venous outflow. Lesions historically referred to as cavernous hemangiomas were previously included in this group but are now recognized as venous malformations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r4">[4]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>This hemodynamic framework assists in determining the threshold for intervention. It facilitates planning of adjunctive procedures such as preoperative embolization and intraoperative use of sclerosing agents to improve procedural safety and predictability.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>ISSVA Classification</p>
        <p>With advances in imaging, interventional radiology, and molecular biology, the International Society for the Study of Vascular Anomalies (ISSVA) developed a standardized nomenclature system incorporating clinical behavior, natural history, hemodynamic properties, radiologic features, and histopathology. This classification was established at the 20th ISSVA Workshop in 2014 and later expanded in 2018 to include biological and genetic updates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <p>Under the ISSVA system, vascular anomalies are broadly categorized as vasoproliferative tumors and vascular malformations, providing a unified framework that supports accurate diagnosis, interprofessional communication, and evidence-based management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Subclassification of Vasoproliferative Tumors</bold>
</p>
        <p>Vasoproliferative tumors are classified according to biological behavior as benign, locally aggressive, or malignant. Benign vasoproliferative tumors include infantile hemangioma, the most common vascular tumor of infancy. Infantile hemangiomas may be superficial, deep, mixed (superficial and deep), or reticular or abortive forms characterized by minimal growth. Lesions may present with focal, multifocal, segmental, or indeterminate patterns that combine focal and segmental features. Infantile hemangiomas may also be associated with systemic syndromes, including PHACE syndrome (Posterior fossa malformations, Hemangioma, Arterial anomalies, Cardiovascular anomalies, Eye anomalies, and sternal clefting or supraumbilical raphe) and LUMBAR syndrome (Lower body hemangioma, Urogenital anomalies, Ulceration, Myelopathy, Bony deformities, Anorectal malformations, Arterial anomalies, and Renal anomalies). Congenital hemangiomas are fully developed at birth and are subclassified as rapidly involuting congenital hemangiomas (RICH), non-involuting congenital hemangiomas (NICH), or partially involuting congenital hemangiomas (PICH). Other benign vascular tumors include tufted angioma, epithelioid hemangioma, spindle cell hemangioma, pyogenic granuloma, and less common variants.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Locally aggressive vasoproliferative tumors</bold> include lesions with intermediate malignant potential, such as hemangioendothelioma and Kaposi sarcoma. These tumors demonstrate locally invasive behavior and a variable clinical course and often require multidisciplinary evaluation and management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Malignant vasoproliferative tumors</bold> include angiosarcoma and malignant epithelioid variants of hemangioendothelioma, among others. These tumors are characterized by aggressive behavior, a high risk of metastasis, and poor overall prognosis.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Subclassification of Vascular Malformations</bold> Vascular malformations are classified according to the type and combination of involved vascular channels. Simple vascular malformations involve a single vascular component and include capillary malformations, venous malformations (VM), lymphatic malformations, arteriovenous malformations (AVM), and arteriovenous fistulas (AVF). Lymphatic malformations may be macrocystic, microcystic, or mixed, while AVMs and AVFs are categorized as high-flow lesions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Combined vascular malformations</bold> consist of&#x000a0;2 or more vascular components within a single lesion. For example, lesions containing capillary, lymphatic, and arteriovenous components are termed capillary-lymphatic-arteriovenous malformations (CLAVM), while lesions composed of venous and lymphatic components are classified as venous-lymphatic malformations (VLM). Historically used terms such as &#x0201c;cavernous hemangioma&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;lymphangioma&#x0201d; should be avoided, as these lesions represent vascular malformations rather than true neoplasms. Lymphangiomas are more accurately classified as venous-lymphatic malformations, and cavernous hemangioma refers to a solitary, encapsulated venous malformation. Similarly, the terms &#x0201c;orbital varix&#x0201d; and &#x0201c;capillary hemangioma&#x0201d; should be avoided in contemporary clinical practice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical staging of arteriovenous malformations</bold> was introduced by Schobinger in 1990 and remains widely used. Stage I represents the quiescent phase and is characterized by warm, pink-blue lesions. Stage II is marked by expansion with pulsation, thrill, and bruit. Stage III involves tissue destruction, including ulceration, bleeding, and dystrophic skin changes. Stage IV represents decompensation, with high-output cardiac failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r7">[7]</xref></p>
        <p>Anomalies of major named vessels, also referred to as channel-type or truncal vascular malformations, involve large-caliber lymphatic, arterial, or venous structures and present as fixed anatomic abnormalities. Syndromic vascular malformations include Klippel-Trenaunay syndrome, Parks-Weber syndrome, Servelle-Martorell syndrome, and Sturge-Weber syndrome. These conditions are characterized by complex vascular anomalies, systemic or developmental abnormalities, and often require coordinated, multidisciplinary care.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Orbital vascular anomalies (OVAs) account for approximately 10% of all orbital pathologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r8">[8]</xref> In a large study conducted in 2011, approximately one-fourth of cases of all orbital masses comprised &#x0201c;vasculogenic lesions,&#x0201d; whereas, in another series, the incidence was found to be 12%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r9">[9]</xref> However, the studies did not use the most recent ISSVA guidelines for vascular anomalies and thus may have resulted in a gross underestimation. It is, therefore, hypothesized that OVAs might be the most common pathology found within the orbit.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>The rationale for the existing gender-specific differences among vascular anomalies remains unclear. For example, infantile hemangioma is&#x000a0;5 times more common in females than males, whereas congenital hemangioma affects both genders equally. Prematurity, low birth weight, and positive family history are the other predisposing factors of infantile hemangioma.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r11">[11]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Cavernous venous malformations (CVMs), the most common benign orbital lesions in adults, are more frequently observed in middle-aged females (20&#x000a0;to 40 years). On the other hand, distensible orbital venous malformations do not have any gender predilection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s5" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>It is imperative to obtain a detailed history and perform a meticulous clinical examination to establish the diagnosis and determine the appropriate management.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemangioma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hemangiomas are the most common vascular tumors of infancy and childhood, frequently affecting the head and neck region. They can be congenital or infantile (formerly termed juvenile hemangioma).</p>
        <p>Infantile hemangioma, the most common type, clinically arises in the first&#x000a0;8 weeks and may be present at birth. Parents typically notice it later, when the baby is a few weeks old. A rapid proliferative phase ensues for 6&#x000a0;to 12 months, followed by an involuting, regressive phase lasting 5 to 9 years. During the proliferative phase, the tumor presents as a raised, rubbery, and red lesion, previously termed &#x0201c;strawberry hemangioma.&#x0201d; The involuted phase is characterized by scarring, wrinkling, and telangiectasia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r14">[14]</xref> One must look for mechanical ptosis in cases of infantile hemangioma, which may result in refractive and stimulus-deprivation amblyopia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Focal infantile hemangioma is the most common variety, presenting as a raised, localized lesion along the area of embryological fusion. On the other hand, segmental&#x000a0;infantile hemangioma presents as flat plaques with a characteristic geographic distribution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r16">[16]</xref> The different types of infantile hemangioma, the patterns exhibited, and the various syndromic associations have been described above.</p>
        <p>Congenital hemangioma presents as full-blown lesions at birth with no proliferative phase. They are less likely to regress spontaneously. RICH, as the name suggests, undergoes a rapid phase of regression and disappears by 1&#x000a0;to 1.5 years&#x000a0;of age. RICH may either present flat violaceous lesions or plaques with coarse telangiectasia or as raised greyish lesions, surrounded by a pale halo and multiple telangiectatic vessels. On the contrary, NICH doesn&#x02019;t show regression and continues to increase in proportion to the child&#x02019;s growth.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Venous Malformations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Orbital venous malformations can be distensible or non-distensible. Colletti G et al in 2018, have re-classified OVMs into&#x000a0;3 subcategories:</p>
        <p>Type 1: It is a low-flow, non-distensible OVM, wrongly termed a &#x0201c;cavernous hemangioma.&#x0201d;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r18">[18]</xref> According to the recently updated ISSVA nomenclature, it is better known as &#x0201c;cavernous venous malformation (CVM).&#x0201d; CVMs are prone to involve the middle third of the orbit and are primarily located in the intraconal space.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r19">[19]</xref> Almost&#x000a0;two-thirds of patients are symptomatic, while the remaining third of cases are diagnosed coincidentally on imaging. Clinical features include gradual-onset axial proptosis, blurred vision, pain, or diplopia. The causes of visual impairment are hyperopic shift (correctable) and compressive optic neuropathy. Hyperopia in these patients is attributable to the lesion's flattening of the globe. It may also manifest as choroidal folds, disc changes ranging from swelling to optic atrophy, visual field defects, abnormal color vision, and afferent pupillary defects. Limitations of extraocular movements and ocular misalignment at primary gaze are observed in up to 30% of patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;Gaze-evoked amaurosis (GEA) is one of the most unusual symptoms of orbital CVM, especially those occupying the extra- as well as the intra-conal spaces of the orbit. Axonal inhibition and optic nerve ischemia are the most likely causes of GEA.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r22">[22]</xref> Optic nerve compromise and permanent axial length modification render the visual impairment irreversible. Recent reports have suggested a positive influence of hormones, such as estrogen or progesterone, on the clinical progression of CVMs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Type 2: Previously known as &#x0201c;orbital varices&#x0201d; - these are low-flow, distensible venous malformations that typically present during the second&#x000a0;to&#x000a0;third&#x000a0;decade of life. Although they result from a congenital weakness in the wall of the veins leading to dilatation of the valveless veins of the orbit, they remain quiescent during childhood, which is then followed by growth around the pubertal period. The presenting clinical features include proptosis, diplopia, visual disturbances, or ecchymosis (due to hemorrhage or thrombosis). Almost 60% of distensible OVMs demonstrate distension on the dependent position or the Valsalva maneuver. The remaining 40%&#x000a0;are usually the more posterior, deeper lesions and hence, show distensibility only on Valsalva-augmented imaging. Distensibility refers to an increase in proptosis due to increased intra-orbital venous pressure and the resultant expansion of the lesion. Hence, clinical evaluation of every suspected case is incomplete without examination at rest, in the dependent position, and during the Valsalva maneuver.</p>
        <p>Type 3: OVMs may be infiltrative, affecting the surrounding periorbital and the intracranial venous system. They classically present as enophthalmos at rest. Significant proptosis can result from straining, coughing, or the Valsalva maneuver, with varying degrees of visual impairment.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r6"/></p>
        <p>
<bold>Lymphatic Malformations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Pure lymphatic malformations of the orbit are rare, as a variable venous component coexists in most cases. They consist of dilated lymphatic channels lined by podoplanin-positive endothelium. Lymphatic malformations are present since birth and can be radiologically classified as microcystic (&#x0003c;2 cm), macrocystic (&#x0003e;2 cm), or mixed, based on the size of the cavity within the lesion. Pure lymphatic malformations can present as an isolated, irregular mass in the anterior orbit. They don&#x02019;t distend on a Valsalva maneuver and cannot be compressed.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Combined Venolymphatic Malformations</bold>
<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>
</p>
        <p>As mentioned above, most lymphatic malformations either have a linked venous component or consist of arterioles and venules that run within the intercystic septa and hence, are often mixed. Studies have shown that the more the superficial location in the orbit, the more predominant the lymphatic component, and vice versa.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Microcystic lymphatic malformations typically infiltrate the tissue and present as diffuse thickening of the eyelid, conjunctiva, or anterior orbit. They can also affect the posterior orbit, sometimes extending till the apex, and manifest as gradual-onset proptosis. Bleeding into the lymphatic channels may lead to the formation of &#x0201c;chocolate cysts,&#x0201d; thereby exerting a mass effect. Sudden worsening of proptosis may result from bleeding within the lesion or from an upper respiratory infection of viral etiology that upregulates the lymphatic system and, in turn, causes rapid expansion of the lymphatic plexus. Massive hemorrhage may lead to motility limitation and visual impairment. Macrocystic lymphatic malformations are typically intraconal and, unlike microcystic lymphatic malformations, are less prone to intralesional hemorrhage. Ocular complications include corneal exposure, astigmatism, strabismus, optic neuropathy, and secondary glaucoma.</p>
        <p>The term "lymphangioma" is incorrect and is often misused in everyday practice. Firstly, this term does not accurately reflect the lesion's histopathological nature, which, in addition to lymphatic channels, includes venous components, smooth muscle fibers, blood products, and lymphocytic tissue. Secondly, unlike hemangioma, lymphangi<italic toggle="yes">oma </italic>is not a proliferative neoplastic lesion.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Arteriovenous Malformations&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <p>These lesions are characterized by rapid arterial flow into the draining venous channels, thereby bypassing the orbit's entire vascular system. Clinically, they present as pulsating proptosis. Thrombosis may manifest as an acute swelling, and hemorrhage may result in ecchymosis. AVMs may also elicit a bruit and cause pain and discomfort either at rest or when distended on the Valsalva maneuver or straining. Although rare, AVMs are invasive, depict an aggressive natural course, and tend to grow exponentially when treated inappropriately. Schobinger&#x02019;s classification (as described above) not only provides insights into the clinical staging but also guides the management of these notorious lesions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Arteriovenous Fistulas (AVFs)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Orbital AVFs, which are usually congenital arterio-venous connections fed by the branches of the ophthalmic artery with an absent intervening bed of capillaries, interspersed within a cellular stroma, clinically manifest as periocular swelling, dilated veins on the fundus, pulsations, and bruit, which typically reduce on compressing the ipsilateral common carotid artery (CCA), glaucoma, and visual deficits. In acquired AVFs, the anomalous connection is either due to trauma or idiopathic. The most common periocular AVF is the carotid-cavernous fistula (CCF). It can be of&#x000a0;2 types: direct and indirect.</p>
        <p>In direct CCF, trauma results in the formation of abnormal direct connections between the internal carotid artery (ICA) and the cavernous sinus. Other causes include Ehler-Danlos syndrome type IV, or iatrogenic procedures such as endarterectomy, trans-sphenoidal resection of the tumor in the pituitary region, percutaneous intervention in trigeminal neuralgia, and maxillofacial surgery. On the other hand, in indirect CCF (also known as a dural fistula), low-flow connections between the dural vein and internal or external carotid artery are formed due to an underlying systemic disease. The connections may involve the meningeal branch of the ICA, branches of the external carotid artery (ECA), or meningeal branches of both the ICA and the ECA.</p>
        <p>Among the branches of ECA, the internal maxillary artery is the most implicated vessel. The natural clinical course is usually indolent in cases of dural fistulas and acute in high-flow CCFs. The presenting features depend upon whether the fistula is high-flow or low-flow. The presence of pulsating proptosis and an objective bruit is a more specific feature of high-flow CCFs. Other clinical features include arterialization of vessels, increased episcleral venous pressure, neurogenic strabismus, most commonly 6th nerve palsy, owing to the central location of the 6th nerve adjacent to the ICA within the substance of the cavernous sinus, optic neuropathy, conjunctival chemosis, and congestion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s6" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Imaging forms a critical component of the diagnostic evaluation of OVAs and plays a crucial role in treatment planning. The advent of interventional radiology has strongly influenced the management and yielded favorable outcomes. The use of modern technology has also obviated the need to perform a biopsy (for diagnostic purposes) on every suspected lesion.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemangioma</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evaluating whether the hemangioma is congenital or infantile is of paramount importance to determine the course of management. Investigations are needed to exclude the differential diagnosis of vascular rhabdomyosarcoma (RMS) and other childhood malignancies. Arterial flow can be visualized on Color Doppler Ultrasonography (USG). Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) reveals a lesion that is slightly hypointense relative to the extraocular muscles on the T1-weighted (T1W) sequence, markedly enhances with contrast, and appears hyperintense on the T2-weighted (T2W) sequence. Involution-related and proliferative changes can be demonstrated on magnetic resonance imaging.</p>
        <p>Diffusion-weighted imaging (DWI) MRI has recently gained popularity due to its ability to qualitatively and quantitatively differentiate benign from malignant lesions using apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) values. In infantile hemangioma, a bright ADC map reveals increased diffusion, whereas RMS shows restricted diffusion with lower diffusion coefficients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r27">[27]</xref> On computed tomography, a hemangioma appears as a hyperdense lesion with homogeneous contrast enhancement. Expansion of the orbital cavity can be appreciated on imaging in case of large lesions, whereas small lesions produce scalloping of the bone adjacent to them.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Vascular Malformations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cavernous Venous Malformation (CVM)</bold>
</p>
        <p>USG (A- and B-scans) is a useful, noninvasive imaging modality, particularly for initial evaluation. A regular, round-shaped lesion with high internal reflectivity and moderate attenuation of echoes is characteristic of CVM. On&#x000a0;computed tomography scan, CVM appears as a well-circumscribed, hyperdense, homogenous, intraconal lesion, seen as a discrete mass that does not directly adhere to the extra-ocular muscles and optic nerve and may occasionally erode the bone. Rarely can CVM encroach on the orbital apex, resembling the typical pear shape. Phleboliths, which are pathognomonic of venous malformations and are caused by chronic inflammation in long-standing cases, may appear&#x000a0;as hyperdense, calcified areas within the mass. On Contrast-Enhancement, only minimal to mild contrast enhancement is seen in the early arterial phase, followed by diffuse enhancement during the late venous phase. This is because the blood flow within the lesion is independent of the orbital vasculature and is predominantly stagnant. Computed tomography angiography shows a nodular pattern of contrast enhancement in the early phase, attributable to small vessels within the lesion. However, radiation-associated ocular complications, mainly lens-related, limit its use as a routine procedure. CVM shows hypointense or isointense signals on T1-weighted (T1W) MRI sequences and heterogeneous hyperintensity on T2-weighted (T2W) MRI, due to intervening areas of thrombosis devoid of hyperintensity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Dynamic investigations, such as Valsalva-augmented MRI, reveal no significant changes in the features. Magnetic resonance angiography (MRA) is a non-invasive technique that provides direct static visualization of the orbital vasculature and indirectly yields limited information about blood flow characteristics.&#x000a0;Dynamic assessment can be achieved using 3D time-of-flight and phase-contrast MRA. It is usually utilized to assess venous flow as a high-flow arterial component causes phase-related artifacts. Although 3D phase-contrast MRA better demonstrates flow characteristics, it is far more susceptible to motion artifacts than 2D MRA owing to its longer acquisition time.</p>
        <p>Thus, to overcome the above challenge, time-resolved imaging of contrast kinetics (TRICKS) was introduced in 2003-04, enabling rapid acquisition of dynamic images of contrast flow with excellent temporal and spatial resolution. TRICKS MRA is a non-invasive, sensitive procedure for the dynamic assessment of venous and arterial flow. In addition to diagnosis, it aids surgical planning and subsequent monitoring. CVM shows negligible and markedly delayed blushing, representing capsular enhancement, on TRICKS.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>CVMs appear relatively hyperintense on DWI, with ADC values ranging from 1.23 &#x000d7; 10<sup>-3</sup> mm<sup>2</sup>/s to 1.39 &#x000d7; 10<sup>-3</sup> mm<sup>2</sup>/s. This should be differentiated from lymphoma, which shows restricted diffusion and, hence, low signal intensity on ADC maps, with ADC values ranging from 0.44 x 10 mm/s to 0.92 x 10 mm/s.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;Nuclear imaging techniques, such as Technetium Tc-99 m&#x02013;labeled red blood cell scintigraphy, are safe, cost-effective, and highly efficacious for identifying these tumors and differentiating them from non-vascular tumors. Orbital phlebography and arteriography have also been studied but are no longer used in current practice.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Venous and Lymphatic Malformations</bold>
</p>
        <p>MRI is the investigation of choice for evaluating and guiding the treatment of vascular malformations. Imaging characteristics mainly depend upon the internal composition of the lesion of interest.</p>
        <p>Distensible venous malformations (OVM Type &#x02013; 2, previously known as &#x0201c;varix&#x0201d;) appear isointense on T1W MRI and hyperintense on T2W MRI. They show vivid enhancement on gadolinium contrast injection. High ADC values greater than 2.8 mm/s on DWI MRI are 100% sensitive and specific for Type&#x02013;2 OVM.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r31">[31]</xref> Phleboliths are typically hypointense on imaging. TRICKS sequences facilitate understanding the dynamic flow of venous malformations.</p>
        <p>Lymphatic malformations comprise micro- or macrocysts, often demonstrating fluid-fluid levels on imaging. Only the solid component, represented by the intercystic septae, enhances on contrast, resulting in an irregular, patchy appearance on imaging. MRI is more effective than&#x000a0;computed tomography in demonstrating recent and old bleed and the nature of fluid within the cyst. As opposed to venous malformations, direct contrast injection into these lesions doesn&#x02019;t demonstrate any outflow.&#x000a0;Combined malformations may exhibit an anterior lymphatic component and a deep-seated, distensible venous component.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>If a lesion in a child extends beyond the eyelids, it is important to evaluate the cheek and the roof of the mouth to rule out coexisting anomalies in these areas. CTA or CE-MRI may be performed to exemplify the extra-orbital components with a special focus on the cheek region and pterygopalatine fossa. If the superior orbital fissure is enlarged, it is vital to look for co-existing vascular anomalies of the brain and skull.</p>
        <p>
<bold>A-V Malformations (AVMs)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Outpatient evaluation of a CCF should include intra-ocular pressure assessment, exophthalmometry, pneumotonometry, and color doppler ultrasonography. A difference in ocular pulse amplitude (OPA) of more than 1.6mm between the eyes is 100% sensitive and 93% specific for CCFs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>On standard computed tomography and MRI scanning, AVMs are identified by the presence of cavernous sinus enlargement with lateral wall convexity, dilated superior ophthalmic vein, and an increase in extraocular muscle thickness. The presence of multiple flow voids, which correspond to high-flow vessels, is a characteristic feature on MRI. The TRICKS sequences help delineate the arterial and venous components as a function of time. AVMs appear irregularly tortuous and show vivid contrast enhancement with or without intracranial involvement. Aneurysms are usually located at the origin of the ophthalmic artery and, rarely, within the orbit, and can be detected on imaging.</p>
        <p>Digital subtraction angiography, in which contrast is directly injected into the feeding vessels and then digitally acquired, is the gold standard for evaluating AVMs. It effectively maps the entire vasculature by delineating the draining venous component and arterial feeder vessels. DSA is both diagnostic and therapeutic. Non-invasive neuroimaging modalities, including&#x000a0;computed tomography angiography and magnetic resonance angiography, are highly sensitive for CCFs. High-flow fistulas demonstrate rapid sinus filling with minimal to absent filling of intracranial vessels. In contrast, low-flow dural fistulas show delayed, slow filling with the intact filling of the intracranial vasculature. Demonstration of nidus on angiography almost always ascertains the diagnosis of an arterio-venous malformation and is typically absent in cases of arteriovenous fistula.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r33">[33]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s7" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>An accurate approach to diagnosing orbital vascular lesions is pivotal for determining the most appropriate management option.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hemangioma</bold>
</p>
        <p>As discussed above, recognizing infantile hemangioma and distinguishing it from congenital hemangioma is vital, as the preferred treatment differs. Medical management, primarily beta-blockers, namely oral propranolol and topical timolol maleate, forms the mainstay of treatment of hemangioma. The child must undergo a comprehensive cardiac evaluation before initiation of therapy. According to the infantile hemangioma guidelines, a trial of atenolol therapy may be considered in patients for whom propranolol is contraindicated or not tolerated. A gel-forming solution of timolol is as effective as systemic therapy and is associated with negligible life-threatening complications.</p>
        <p>Before the advent of beta-blocker use, systemic and intralesional steroid therapy was the first line of management. Intralesional sclerosing agents, such as bleomycin, are reserved for patients unresponsive to propranolol therapy and are seldom used as the initial mode of treatment. The role of intralesional bevacizumab is unclear, and the drug is under experimental phases. Lasers are not recommended for infantile hemangioma, except for the treatment of residual skin changes and scars following resolution.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>For congenital hemangioma, there is no proven medical management. Surgical excision, usually performed between ages 2 and 5, remains the first-line treatment. For very large NICH, pre-operative embolization by an interventional neuroradiologist prevents the risk of excessive bleeding during surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r35">[35]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Vascular Malformations</bold>
</p>
        <p>
<bold>Cavernous Venous Malformation (CVM)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Observation and periodic follow-up are encouraged in asymptomatic CVMs.</p>
        <p>Indications for surgical intervention include:</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Symptomatic CVMs</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Visual loss due to optic nerve compression</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Disfiguring proptosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Dystopia&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r36">[36]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The surgical approach primarily depends on the lesion's anatomic location and its relationship to surrounding structures.</p>
        <p>Anterior and lateral orbitotomy are performed for malformations not involving the orbital apex.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r38">[38]</xref>&#x000a0;Lesions in the posterior third of the orbit can be accessed via additional neurosurgical approaches, such as transcranial or combined anterior and transnasal endoscopic routes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r39">[39]</xref> Techniques that assist in excision include cryoextraction using an ophthalmic cryoprobe, stereotactic radiotherapy, or preoperative embolization in cases where CVM invades bone. Percutaneous sclerotherapy has also been described as a minimally invasive treatment reserved for patients unwilling to undergo surgery or in cases where iatrogenic risks outweigh the benefits of intervention. Intralesional injection of pingyangmycin (bleomycin A5) significantly reduces intralesional volume and provides pain and proptosis relief.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r40">[40]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Venous and Lymphatic Malformations</bold>
</p>
        <p>The therapy goals in venous malformations are to reduce the symptoms (persistent pain), improve function (optic neuropathy and corneal exposure leading to visual loss, tense hemorrhage), and achieve acceptable cosmesis. Management options for orbital venous malformations are as follows:<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r6">[6]</xref></p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Observation: for asymptomatic/minimally symptomatic cases</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Surgery: for isolated, localized lesions where complete excision is feasible with minimal morbidity. Surgical can be performed to debulk an extensive lesion or as an adjunct to sclerotherapy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Sclerotherapy: for extensive facial involvement and lesions not amenable to surgical resection</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Intra-operative mapping and glue (n-butyl cyanoacrylate) embolization aid in surgical excision, thereby reducing the risk of rupture and troublesome bleeding.&#x000a0;Macrocystic lymphatic malformations respond well to sclerotherapy, whereas microcystic lymphatic malformations, in addition to sclerotherapy, may require surgical intervention. The most commonly used sclerosant is aqueous bleomycin solution, reconstituted with lignocaine and adrenaline.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>Microcystic lymphatic malformations also respond well to drugs like sirolimus. If microcystic lymphatic malformations extend into the orbital apex, the lesion can be truncated to avoid the risk of functional damage following complete excision. Surgical decapsulation of cysts may occasionally be performed for hemorrhagic macrocystic lesions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>A-V Malformations (AVMs)</bold>
</p>
        <p>A crisp understanding of the hemodynamics of AVMs facilitates decision-making regarding the appropriate management strategy. A combined approach of preoperative embolization followed by resection of the nidus is the preferred management strategy. Caution should be maintained to completely resect the nidus to prevent recurrence, as a recurrent AVM grows exponentially and is more cumbersome to treat. Not all AVMs are amenable to resection. Some of these can be managed conservatively as they may carry significant surgical morbidity and iatrogenic risk of damaging surrounding structures. Specific indications of surgical excision include recurrent hemorrhage, pain, and associated visual disturbances.</p>
        <p>Successful closure of high-flow direct CCFs can be achieved with minimal morbidity using recently emerged endovascular techniques. Endovascular embolization via the transarterial route into the cavernous sinus, using materials such as detachable balloon, n-butyl cyanoacrylate, or ethyl vinyl alcohol polymer, is the preferred management strategy. Recently, due to the non-availability of detachable balloons, coiling has emerged as the treatment modality of choice.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;In cases with an associated ICA wall tear, flow-diverting stent-assisted embolization is a viable option because it avoids misdirection of material into the arterial circulation, thereby preventing life-threatening complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r45">[45]</xref> Endovascular intervention in high-flow CCFs has shown 90-100% success rates with low (&#x0003c;1%) mortality.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r46">[46]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r47">[47]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r48">[48]</xref></p>
        <p>Dural fistulas are frequently managed conservatively, as approximately 70% close spontaneously due to localized thrombosis of the superior ophthalmic vein. In the absence of high-risk features, a conservative approach is the first-line treatment, including observation, intermittent carotid compression, or IOP-lowering agents.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r50">[50]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r51">[51]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>While performing a carotid massage, it is advisable to use the contralateral hand to compress the ipsilateral ICA. This is because, if cerebral ischemia results from reduced arterial pressure, contralateral hemiparesis itself reduces the pressure on the artery, thereby creating a feedback loop. Carotid compression in the presence of atherosclerotic disease further aggravates the risk of stroke due to pre-existing carotid insufficiency. The additional technique involves superomedial SOV compression in these cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r26">[26]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Indications of Intervention in Dural AVFs</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="order">
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent diplopia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Uncontrollable IOP</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe proptosis with exposure keratopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Optic neuropathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Ischemic retinopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cortical venous drainage from the fistula</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Severe bruit (rare)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>The endovascular approach to dural AVFs is similar to that for high-flow CCFs.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s8" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of orbital vascular anomalies depends upon whether the mass is intraconal or extraconal, the nature of the lesion, and its relative localization within the orbit. The differential diagnoses for a well-circumscribed solid tumor include cavernous venous malformation, schwannoma, solitary fibrous tumor, lymphoid lesions of the orbit (lymphoma), benign reactive hyperplasia, orbital metastasis, fibrous histiocytoma, or neurofibroma.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s9" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>Every orbital vascular anomaly behaves distinctly. The prognosis depends primarily on the nature, extent, and localization of the lesion, visual acuity, and the status of the optic nerve.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s10" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Lesions extending to the orbital apex, large-sized anomalies, and those associated with optic neuropathy are associated with a higher risk of vision-related complications following surgery. Other possible complications include ptosis and motility limitations, which usually resolve within a few months. Recurrence is not uncommon. The risk of amblyopia should be a key consideration when managing childhood vascular anomalies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-140354.r52">[52]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s11" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Patients and their family members must be counseled and encouraged to follow up promptly with their treating ophthalmologist, pediatrician, and the associated radiologist. To make informed decisions, patients must be educated about the nature of the lesion, possible complications, and the risks and benefits of the recommended intervention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s12" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Management of orbital vascular anomalies is complex and challenging. A team-driven approach is recommended to avoid misdiagnosis and mismanagement of OVAs. Interprofessional collaboration among ophthalmologists specializing in orbit, an interventional neuroradiologist, and a pediatrician is essential. Shared decision-making, coordination, and effective communication are critical to maximizing favorable outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-140354.s13">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
